Each string is one line, and a run starts one container for each line. The line's words replace the image's CMD, so the program receives them as its arguments. The template's two lines start these two containers: python -u /app/main.py -t 10 out.txt
python -u /app/main.py -t 20 --label 'long run' out.txt Maestro splits a line into words at spaces. Single quotes, double quotes and backslashes keep words together the way they do in a shell, so 'long run' reaches the program as one argument. Nothing else is special, because no shell runs the line. A $HOME or a * reaches the program as typed. A program that needs a shell can be a shell script. Add a third line, and the next build says 3 lines: parameters = [ "-t 10 out.txt", "-t 20 --label 'long run' out.txt", "-t 5 --label quick out.txt",
] A run has at most 5000 lines. When a list gets long or follows a pattern, a generator can compute it.
